module.exports = {
  title: 'Tooling, Testing & Ecosystem',
  slug: 'tooling-testing',
  order: 9,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Vite and the Build Pipeline',
      slug: 'vite-tooling',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Vite is the default build tool for Vue, written by Vue's creator, and "why is Vite faster than Webpack?" is a common interview question with a genuinely interesting answer — it is an architectural difference, not an optimisation. Being able to explain native ES modules in development versus bundling, and why the two halves of Vite use different tools, shows you understand your toolchain rather than just running <code>npm run dev</code>.</p>`,
        `<p>Practically, the build config is where environment variables, path aliases, proxying and chunking live — all things you will touch in the first week of any project.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vite has <strong>two different modes</strong>, and the split is the whole idea:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Development</strong> — no bundling. Vite serves your source files over native ES modules and lets the browser request what it needs. Starting the server is near-instant regardless of project size.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Production</strong> — a full Rollup bundle, because shipping hundreds of unbundled module requests to real users would be slow.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch10-vite-vs-webpack.svg|Comparison of a bundler that must build the whole dependency graph before serving anything, versus Vite serving source files over native ESM and transforming each on demand|Webpack bundles before it can serve; Vite serves immediately and transforms each module as the browser asks for it.}}`,
        `<p>That is why the dev server starts in milliseconds on a project where Webpack takes thirty seconds: Webpack has to crawl and bundle the entire dependency graph before it can serve anything, while Vite serves <code>index.html</code> straight away and transforms each module on demand as the browser requests it.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Development.</strong> The browser requests <code>/src/main.js</code>; Vite transforms it — compiling the SFC, stripping TypeScript types — and returns it, rewriting bare imports like <code>import { ref } from 'vue'</code> to real paths the browser can fetch. Work is proportional to what you actually open, not to project size.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Dependency pre-bundling.</strong> One wrinkle: <code>node_modules</code> packages are often CommonJS, or split into hundreds of small files (lodash-es is ~600 modules, which would be 600 requests). Vite pre-bundles dependencies once with <strong>esbuild</strong> — Go-based, 10–100× faster than a JavaScript bundler — converting to ESM and merging into single files, then caches the result. Only your own source is transformed on demand.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>HMR</strong> is fast for the same architectural reason. When a file changes, Vite invalidates only that module and its importers, and the browser refetches just that. There is no bundle to rebuild, so HMR stays fast as the project grows — the property that matters most day to day.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why esbuild for dependencies and Rollup for production?</strong> esbuild is far faster but its code-splitting and plugin ecosystem are less mature; Rollup produces better production output and has the plugin API the ecosystem relies on. Vite uses each where its strength applies. (Rolldown, a Rust port of Rollup, is intended to unify the two eventually.)</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Environment variables</strong> follow one rule with a security consequence: only <code>VITE_</code>-prefixed variables are exposed to client code, and they are <em>inlined at build time</em>. So they are public, and changing one requires a rebuild.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// vite.config.js — the settings you actually use\nimport { defineConfig, loadEnv } from 'vite'\nimport vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue'\nimport vueJsx from '@vitejs/plugin-vue-jsx'\nimport { fileURLToPath, URL } from 'node:url'\n\nexport default defineConfig(({ mode }) =&gt; {\n  const env = loadEnv(mode, process.cwd(), '')   // '' loads ALL vars, server-side only\n\n  return {\n    plugins: [vue(), vueJsx()],\n\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        '@': fileURLToPath(new URL('./src', import.meta.url)),\n      },\n    },\n\n    server: {\n      port: 5173,\n      // Proxy in development so the browser sees a same-origin API — no CORS\n      proxy: {\n        '/api': {\n          target: env.API_URL || 'http://localhost:3000',\n          changeOrigin: true,\n          rewrite: (path) =&gt; path.replace(/^\\/api/, ''),\n        },\n      },\n    },\n\n    build: {\n      sourcemap: mode !== 'production',\n      chunkSizeWarningLimit: 600,\n      rollupOptions: {\n        output: {\n          manualChunks(id) {\n            if (id.includes('node_modules/vue') || id.includes('vue-router')) return 'vue'\n            if (id.includes('node_modules/chart.js')) return 'charts'\n            if (id.includes('node_modules')) return 'vendor'\n          },\n        },\n      },\n    },\n  }\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-bash"># .env                  loaded in every mode\n# .env.local            git-ignored, every mode\n# .env.development      npm run dev\n# .env.production       npm run build\n\nVITE_API_URL=https://api.example.com     # ✅ exposed to the client (PUBLIC)\nVITE_ANALYTICS_ID=UA-123                 # ✅ exposed (fine — publishable)\nDATABASE_PASSWORD=secret                 # ✅ NOT exposed (no VITE_ prefix)\nVITE_STRIPE_SECRET=sk_live_xxx           # ❌ INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE. Never.</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Reading them\nconsole.log(import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL)  // inlined at BUILD time\nconsole.log(import.meta.env.MODE)          // 'development' | 'production'\nconsole.log(import.meta.env.DEV)           // boolean\nconsole.log(import.meta.env.PROD)\n\n// Vite-specific import features worth knowing\nconst modules = import.meta.glob('./views/*.vue')          // lazy: { path: () =&gt; import(...) }\nconst eager = import.meta.glob('./locales/*.json', { eager: true })  // imported immediately\n\n// Static assets: imported for a hashed, cache-busted URL\nimport logoUrl from '@/assets/logo.svg'\n\n// Web workers and raw text\nimport Worker from './heavy.worker.js?worker'\nimport schema from './schema.graphql?raw'</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-json">// package.json — the standard scripts\n{\n  "scripts": {\n    "dev": "vite",\n    "build": "vue-tsc --noEmit &amp;&amp; vite build",\n    "preview": "vite preview",\n    "test": "vitest",\n    "lint": "eslint . --fix"\n  }\n}\n// \`preview\` serves the PRODUCTION build locally — always check here before\n// deploying, since dev and prod differ (minification, chunking, env vars).</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting secrets in <code>VITE_</code> variables.</strong> They are inlined into the bundle and readable by anyone.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting env changes without a rebuild.</strong> They are compile-time constants, not runtime lookups.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Testing only the dev server.</strong> Dev is unbundled and unminified; use <code>vite preview</code> before deploying.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Relying on the dev proxy in production.</strong> It only exists in the dev server; production needs real CORS headers or a reverse proxy.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the <code>base</code> option</strong> when deploying to a subdirectory — every asset URL 404s.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming Webpack loaders work.</strong> Vite uses Rollup plugins; most have equivalents but the config is different.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Importing CommonJS-only packages carelessly.</strong> Usually handled by pre-bundling, occasionally needs <code>optimizeDeps.include</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not clearing the dep cache after a dependency change.</strong> Stale pre-bundles cause bewildering errors; delete <code>node_modules/.vite</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Skipping <code>vue-tsc</code> in the build script.</strong> Vite strips types without checking them, so type errors reach production unless you check separately.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Scaffold with <code>npm create vue@latest</code> — it wires TypeScript, router, Pinia, Vitest and ESLint in a tested configuration.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the <code>@</code> alias for <code>src</code> and keep imports absolute past one level of nesting.</li>`,
        `<li>Run <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> as part of <code>build</code>, since Vite only strips types.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep real secrets out of the client entirely; only publishable values get a <code>VITE_</code> prefix.</li>`,
        `<li>Use the dev proxy to avoid CORS locally, and mirror the arrangement in production with a real proxy.</li>`,
        `<li>Configure <code>manualChunks</code> deliberately rather than accepting whatever Rollup produces.</li>`,
        `<li>Check <code>vite preview</code> before every deploy.</li>`,
        `<li>Add <code>rollup-plugin-visualizer</code> and look at it occasionally.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is Vite faster than Webpack in development?"</strong><br>A: It doesn't bundle. Webpack has to crawl and bundle the entire dependency graph before it can serve anything, so startup scales with project size. Vite serves <code>index.html</code> immediately and relies on native ES modules — the browser requests each module and Vite transforms it on demand, so work is proportional to what you actually open. HMR is fast for the same reason: only the changed module is invalidated, with no bundle to rebuild, so it stays fast as the project grows.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "If it doesn't bundle, why does it use Rollup?"</strong><br>A: Only for production. Shipping hundreds of unbundled module requests to real users on real networks would be far slower than a bundle, so the production build is a full Rollup build with tree-shaking, minification and code splitting. Vite deliberately uses different tools for different jobs: esbuild for dependency pre-bundling because it's 10–100× faster, and Rollup for production because its output and plugin ecosystem are more mature.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is dependency pre-bundling?"</strong><br>A: Vite runs <code>node_modules</code> dependencies through esbuild once, converting CommonJS to ESM and merging many small files into one. Two reasons: the browser can't import CommonJS, and a package like lodash-es is hundreds of separate modules, which unbundled would mean hundreds of HTTP requests. The result is cached in <code>node_modules/.vite</code>, so it only re-runs when dependencies change — and clearing that directory is the fix when you hit a bewildering stale-module error.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do environment variables work, and what's the trap?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Only <code>VITE_</code>-prefixed variables are exposed to client code, via <code>import.meta.env</code>, and they're <strong>inlined at build time</strong> — they become string literals in the bundle. So two things follow: they're completely public, which makes putting any secret behind a <code>VITE_</code> prefix a real vulnerability; and changing one requires a rebuild, because there's no runtime lookup to update. Unprefixed variables stay server-side and are available in the config via <code>loadEnv</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Does Vite type-check?"</strong><br>A: No — it strips TypeScript types with esbuild for speed and never checks them. That's a deliberate trade-off, but it means type errors compile cleanly and reach production. The standard fix is running <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> as part of the build script and in CI, which type-checks templates too.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Dev = <strong>no bundle</strong>, native ESM, transform on demand. Prod = <strong>Rollup bundle</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Deps pre-bundled once with <strong>esbuild</strong> (CJS→ESM, fewer requests), cached in <code>node_modules/.vite</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>HMR invalidates one module — stays fast as the project grows.</li>`,
        `<li><code>VITE_*</code> vars are <strong>inlined at build time</strong> ⇒ public, and need a rebuild to change.</li>`,
        `<li><code>import.meta.env.MODE / DEV / PROD</code>; <code>loadEnv</code> for server-side config.</li>`,
        `<li><code>import.meta.glob()</code> for lazy or eager bulk imports; <code>?worker</code>, <code>?raw</code>, <code>?url</code> suffixes.</li>`,
        `<li>Dev-only <code>server.proxy</code> avoids CORS locally; production needs a real proxy.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Vite doesn't type-check</strong> → run <code>vue-tsc --noEmit</code> in <code>build</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>vite preview</code> before deploying; set <code>base</code> for subdirectory deploys.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Testing Vue Applications',
      slug: 'testing',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Every mid-level-and-up Vue interview asks about testing, and the question behind the question is <em>what do you test</em>. Candidates who describe asserting on internal state and calling component methods reveal that their tests break on every refactor. Candidates who describe testing behaviour through the rendered output describe tests that survive.</p>`,
        `<p>The Vue-specific mechanics matter too — async updates mean nearly every interaction needs an <code>await</code>, and forgetting that produces confusing failures that look like broken code rather than broken tests.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Three layers, each answering a different question:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Tests</th><th>Tools</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>Unit</strong></td><td>Pure functions, composables, store logic</td><td>Vitest</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>Component</strong></td><td>A component's rendered output and interactions</td><td>Vitest + Vue Test Utils / Testing Library</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><strong>End-to-end</strong></td><td>Real user journeys in a real browser</td><td>Playwright / Cypress</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>The governing principle: <strong>test behaviour, not implementation.</strong> A user does not know your component has a <code>count</code> ref — they know that clicking the button makes the number change. Assert on what is rendered and what happens when you interact, and refactors that preserve behaviour will not break your suite.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch10-testing-pyramid.svg|Testing layers for a Vue app: many fast unit tests of composables and stores, fewer component tests of rendered output and interaction, and a small number of end-to-end journeys|Many cheap unit tests, a solid layer of component tests, a few end-to-end journeys over the critical paths.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Vitest</strong> is the default runner for Vue because it reuses your <code>vite.config.js</code> — the same aliases, plugins and transforms, so a component that builds will test. The API is Jest-compatible, so existing knowledge transfers.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The async rule.</strong> Vue batches DOM updates and flushes on the next microtask, so nothing you assert immediately after a state change reflects it. Vue Test Utils' interaction helpers — <code>trigger</code>, <code>setValue</code>, <code>setProps</code> — return <code>nextTick()</code>, so <code>await</code>ing them is enough. For changes made outside the wrapper, add an explicit <code>await nextTick()</code>. Missing this is the number-one cause of confusing test failures in Vue.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Querying.</strong> Prefer accessible queries — role, label, text — over CSS selectors, because those are what a user perceives and they do not break when class names change. Where a test hook is genuinely needed, <code>data-testid</code> is explicit and stable. Testing Library enforces this style; Vue Test Utils permits either.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>What to mock.</strong> Mock the boundaries — network, time, browser APIs — and as little else as possible. Mocking a child component makes the test faster and more isolated but stops it catching integration mistakes; <code>shallow: true</code> stubs all children, which is occasionally right and often hides real bugs. MSW (Mock Service Worker) intercepts at the network layer, so your code under test uses the real <code>fetch</code> path.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Composables</strong> that use no lifecycle hooks are plain functions and test directly. Ones that call <code>onMounted</code> or <code>inject</code> need a component context — mount a tiny test component that calls the composable and exposes its result.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>E2E</strong> covers what unit and component tests cannot: routing, real network, real browser behaviour, and the interaction between pieces. Keep the set small and focused on critical paths — signup, checkout, the primary workflow — because these are the slowest and most brittle tests you own.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// vitest.config.js — reuses the Vite config\nimport { defineConfig, mergeConfig } from 'vitest/config'\nimport viteConfig from './vite.config'\n\nexport default mergeConfig(viteConfig, defineConfig({\n  test: {\n    environment: 'jsdom',      // a DOM for component tests\n    globals: true,             // describe/it/expect without imports\n    setupFiles: ['./tests/setup.js'],\n    coverage: { reporter: ['text', 'html'], exclude: ['**/*.config.js'] },\n  },\n}))</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Component test — behaviour, not implementation\nimport { mount } from '@vue/test-utils'\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi } from 'vitest'\nimport TodoList from '@/components/TodoList.vue'\n\ndescribe('TodoList', () =&gt; {\n  it('adds a todo when the form is submitted', async () =&gt; {\n    const wrapper = mount(TodoList, {\n      props: { initialTodos: [] },\n    })\n\n    // ⚠️ await — Vue's DOM updates are async. Without it the assertion\n    //    runs against the pre-update DOM and fails confusingly.\n    await wrapper.find('[data-testid=\"new-todo\"]').setValue('Buy milk')\n    await wrapper.find('form').trigger('submit')\n\n    // ✅ Assert on rendered output, not on wrapper.vm.todos\n    expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('Buy milk')\n    expect(wrapper.findAll('[data-testid=\"todo-item\"]')).toHaveLength(1)\n  })\n\n  it('emits remove with the todo id', async () =&gt; {\n    const wrapper = mount(TodoList, {\n      props: { initialTodos: [{ id: 1, text: 'Existing' }] },\n    })\n\n    await wrapper.find('[data-testid=\"remove\"]').trigger('click')\n\n    expect(wrapper.emitted('remove')).toBeTruthy()\n    expect(wrapper.emitted('remove')[0]).toEqual([1])\n  })\n\n  it('reacts to a prop change', async () =&gt; {\n    const wrapper = mount(TodoList, { props: { initialTodos: [] } })\n    await wrapper.setProps({ initialTodos: [{ id: 2, text: 'New' }] })\n    expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('New')\n  })\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Testing Library — pushes you toward accessible, user-centric queries\nimport { render, screen } from '@testing-library/vue'\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event'\n\nit('lets a user log in', async () =&gt; {\n  const user = userEvent.setup()\n  render(LoginForm)\n\n  // Queried the way a user perceives the page\n  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText('Email'), 'ada@example.com')\n  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText('Password'), 'secret')\n  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /sign in/i }))\n\n  expect(await screen.findByText('Welcome back')).toBeInTheDocument()\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Testing a composable\nimport { withSetup } from './helpers'   // small helper mounting a dummy component\nimport { useCounter } from '@/composables/useCounter'\n\n// ① No lifecycle hooks → it's just a function\nit('increments', () =&gt; {\n  const { count, increment } = useCounter(5)\n  increment()\n  expect(count.value).toBe(6)\n})\n\n// ② Uses onMounted/inject → needs a component context\n// helpers.js\nimport { createApp } from 'vue'\nexport function withSetup(composable) {\n  let result\n  const app = createApp({\n    setup() { result = composable(); return () =&gt; {} },\n  })\n  app.mount(document.createElement('div'))\n  return [result, app]      // app.unmount() to trigger cleanup hooks\n}\n\nit('registers and cleans up a listener', () =&gt; {\n  const spy = vi.spyOn(window, 'addEventListener')\n  const [, app] = withSetup(() =&gt; useMouse())\n  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith('mousemove', expect.any(Function))\n  app.unmount()             // onUnmounted runs here\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Mocking at the boundaries\nimport { vi } from 'vitest'\n\n// ① A module\nvi.mock('@/services/api', () =&gt; ({\n  getUser: vi.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: 1, name: 'Ada' }),\n}))\n\n// ② Time\nvi.useFakeTimers()\nvi.advanceTimersByTime(1000)\nvi.useRealTimers()\n\n// ③ The router, for a component that uses useRoute/useRouter\nimport { createRouter, createMemoryHistory } from 'vue-router'\nconst router = createRouter({ history: createMemoryHistory(), routes })\nawait router.push('/users/42')\nawait router.isReady()\nmount(Component, { global: { plugins: [router] } })\n\n// ④ Network via MSW — the code under test uses the real fetch path\nimport { setupServer } from 'msw/node'\nimport { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw'\n\nconst server = setupServer(\n  http.get('/api/users/:id', () =&gt; HttpResponse.json({ id: 1, name: 'Ada' })),\n)\nbeforeAll(() =&gt; server.listen())\nafterEach(() =&gt; server.resetHandlers())\nafterAll(() =&gt; server.close())</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// E2E — a critical path, in a real browser\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'\n\ntest('a user can complete checkout', async ({ page }) =&gt; {\n  await page.goto('/products')\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to cart' }).first().click()\n  await page.getByRole('link', { name: /cart/i }).click()\n\n  await expect(page.getByTestId('cart-count')).toHaveText('1')\n\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Checkout' }).click()\n  await page.getByLabel('Card number').fill('4242424242424242')\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Pay' }).click()\n\n  await expect(page.getByText('Order confirmed')).toBeVisible()\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>await</code>.</strong> DOM updates are async; assertions run against the previous render. The single most common Vue testing bug.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Asserting on <code>wrapper.vm</code>.</strong> Testing internal state couples the test to implementation, so every refactor breaks it. Assert on output.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling component methods directly.</strong> Trigger the interaction a user would.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Over-mocking.</strong> Stubbing every child gives a test that passes while the app is broken.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Brittle CSS selectors.</strong> <code>.btn-primary.mt-4</code> breaks on a styling change. Use roles, labels or <code>data-testid</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not resetting state between tests.</strong> Pinia stores and module state leak; use <code>setActivePinia(createPinia())</code> and reset mocks.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>createTestingPinia</code> stubs actions.</strong> Assertions on state an action would change fail because the action never ran.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Snapshot tests of whole components.</strong> They fail on every markup change and nobody reads the diff — so they get regenerated blindly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Too many E2E tests.</strong> Slow, flaky and expensive to maintain. Cover critical paths and push detail down to component tests.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Chasing 100% coverage.</strong> Coverage measures execution, not correctness.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Test behaviour through the rendered output — what a user sees and does.</li>`,
        `<li><code>await</code> every interaction helper, and <code>await nextTick()</code> after external changes.</li>`,
        `<li>Query by role, label or text; fall back to <code>data-testid</code>, never to styling classes.</li>`,
        `<li>Mock at boundaries — network with MSW, time with fake timers, browser APIs explicitly.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>createTestingPinia</code> for components and a real Pinia for store unit tests.</li>`,
        `<li>Test composables directly when they have no lifecycle hooks, and via a tiny host component when they do.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep E2E to critical journeys and run them in CI against a production-like build.</li>`,
        `<li>Write the test that would have caught the bug you just fixed.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat a flaky test as a bug — quarantine and fix it rather than retrying.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you test a Vue component?"</strong><br>A: Mount it with Vue Test Utils or Testing Library, interact the way a user would, and assert on rendered output — not on internal state. So: set an input's value, trigger a submit, then check the text appears and the right event was emitted. I avoid <code>wrapper.vm</code> and calling methods directly, because that couples the test to implementation and breaks on refactors that don't change behaviour. And every interaction needs an <code>await</code>, since Vue's DOM updates are batched to the next microtask.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does my assertion fail even though the app works?"</strong><br>A: Almost always a missing <code>await</code>. Vue queues updates and flushes on the next microtask, so immediately after <code>trigger('click')</code> the DOM still shows the previous render. Vue Test Utils' helpers return <code>nextTick()</code>, so awaiting them is enough; for state changed outside the wrapper — a store mutation, say — you need an explicit <code>await nextTick()</code>.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What do you mock, and what don't you?"</strong><br>A: Boundaries only — network, time, browser APIs. For network I prefer MSW, which intercepts at the HTTP layer so the code under test still uses the real fetch path, rather than stubbing my own service module and testing nothing about how it's called. I avoid stubbing child components: it makes tests faster but they stop catching integration mistakes, which is usually where the bugs are. <code>shallow: true</code> is a red flag for me unless there's a specific reason.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you test a composable?"</strong><br>A: If it has no lifecycle hooks, it's a plain function — call it and assert on the refs it returns. If it uses <code>onMounted</code>, <code>onUnmounted</code> or <code>inject</code>, it needs an active component instance, so I mount a minimal test component whose setup calls the composable and exposes the result. That also gives me a way to test cleanup: unmount the app and assert the listener was removed.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How much E2E testing?"</strong><br>A: Enough to cover the journeys that would cost money if they broke — signup, login, checkout, the primary workflow. They're the only tests that exercise routing, real network and real browser behaviour together, so they catch a class of bug nothing else can. But they're slow and the most brittle thing you own, so I keep the set small and push detail down into component tests, which are fast enough to run on every save.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Layers: unit (composables/stores) · component (VTU / Testing Library) · E2E (Playwright).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Test behaviour, not implementation</strong> — never <code>wrapper.vm</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>await</code> every interaction</strong> — <code>trigger</code>/<code>setValue</code>/<code>setProps</code> return <code>nextTick()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Query by role/label/text, then <code>data-testid</code>. Never styling classes.</li>`,
        `<li>Vitest reuses <code>vite.config.js</code>; <code>environment: 'jsdom'</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Mock boundaries: <strong>MSW</strong> for network, fake timers for time, memory history for the router.</li>`,
        `<li><code>createTestingPinia</code> (<strong>stubs actions by default</strong>) for components; <code>setActivePinia</code> for store tests.</li>`,
        `<li>Composables: plain call if no hooks; tiny host component if hooks.</li>`,
        `<li>Few E2E tests, critical paths only. Coverage ≠ correctness.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'SSR, Hydration and Nuxt',
      slug: 'ssr-nuxt',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Server-side rendering is where a lot of senior Vue interviews go, because it forces you to reason about code running in two environments with different capabilities. The classic questions — "what is hydration?", "why do hydration mismatches happen?", "why does <code>window</code> break your build?" — all have the same root cause, and a candidate who can name it once explains all of them.</p>`,
        `<p>It also matters commercially: SEO and first-contentful-paint are the reasons SSR exists, and being able to say when it is <em>not</em> worth the complexity is as valuable as knowing how it works.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>In a normal SPA the server sends an almost empty HTML file; the browser downloads JavaScript, runs it, and only then renders anything. With <strong>SSR</strong>, the server runs your Vue app, produces real HTML, and sends that — so the user sees content before any JavaScript has executed.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Hydration</strong> is the second half: the same app boots in the browser, walks the server-rendered DOM, and attaches reactivity and event listeners to the existing elements instead of recreating them. The markup stays; it becomes interactive.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch10-ssr-hydration.svg|Timeline comparing SPA rendering — blank HTML, JS download, then render — with SSR: server-rendered HTML visible immediately, then JS downloads and hydrates the existing markup into an interactive app|SSR moves the first render to the server; hydration attaches the client app to the markup that is already on screen.}}`,
        `<p>The related options are worth naming, because "SSR" is often used loosely:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>SSR</strong> — HTML rendered per request. Right for personalised or frequently changing pages.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>SSG</strong> — HTML rendered at build time. Right for content that changes rarely: docs, marketing, blogs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>ISR</strong> — static, regenerated periodically. A middle ground.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>CSR</strong> — the plain SPA. Right for dashboards behind a login, where SEO is irrelevant.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>The one root cause.</strong> Server code runs in Node, which has no <code>window</code>, no <code>document</code>, no <code>localStorage</code>. Every SSR problem descends from that:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Touching a browser API during setup crashes the render — so browser-only code goes in <code>onMounted</code>, which never runs on the server.</li>`,
        `<li><code>onMounted</code> and <code>onUnmounted</code> do not run server-side, so anything in them is client-only by definition. The other hooks do run, which is why cleanup semantics differ.</li>`,
        `<li>Module-scope state is per <em>process</em>, not per request, so it leaks between users. Everything must be created per request — which is exactly why Pinia stores are instantiated per app.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Hydration mismatches</strong> happen when the client's first render produces different markup from the server's. Vue warns, discards the mismatched subtree and re-renders it, so you lose the performance benefit and can get a visible flash. The usual causes are non-deterministic values (<code>Date.now()</code>, <code>Math.random()</code>), branching on something only available on one side (<code>window.innerWidth</code>, a cookie read client-side), invalid HTML nesting that the browser silently corrects (a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>), and — most commonly — state that was not transferred from server to client.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>State transfer</strong> is therefore mandatory: the server serialises its store state into the HTML, and the client assigns it before mounting, so the first client render matches. Skip it and the server rendered "3 items" while the client starts empty.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Nuxt</strong> exists because doing all of this by hand — a server bundle, a client bundle, per-request app creation, state serialisation, routing on both sides, per-route rendering modes — is a lot of infrastructure. Nuxt provides file-based routing, auto-imports, per-route rendering modes, <code>useAsyncData</code>/<code>useFetch</code> that fetch on the server and transfer the result automatically, and <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> for genuinely browser-only components.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// The shape of a manual SSR setup — what Nuxt does for you\n\n// app.js — a FACTORY, so every request gets a fresh app\nexport function createApp() {\n  const app = createSSRApp(App)      // note: createSSRApp, not createApp\n  const router = createRouter({ history: createMemoryHistory(), routes })\n  const pinia = createPinia()\n  app.use(router).use(pinia)\n  return { app, router, pinia }\n}\n\n// entry-server.js\nimport { renderToString } from 'vue/server-renderer'\nimport devalue from '@nuxt/devalue'\n\nexport async function render(url) {\n  const { app, router, pinia } = createApp()   // per request — never module scope\n  await router.push(url)\n  await router.isReady()\n\n  const html = await renderToString(app)\n  const state = devalue(pinia.state.value)     // handles Date/Map/Set/undefined\n  return { html, state }\n}\n\n// entry-client.js\nconst { app, router, pinia } = createApp()\n\n// ⚠️ Restore state BEFORE mounting, or the first client render won't match\nif (window.__INITIAL_STATE__) pinia.state.value = window.__INITIAL_STATE__\n\nrouter.isReady().then(() =&gt; app.mount('#app'))   // mount() hydrates existing markup</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- The SSR-safe patterns --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onMounted } from 'vue'\n\n// ❌ Crashes on the server — no window in Node\n// const width = window.innerWidth\n// const theme = localStorage.getItem('theme')\n\n// ✅ onMounted never runs on the server, so it's the safe boundary\nconst width = ref(0)\nconst theme = ref('light')\n\nonMounted(() =&gt; {\n  width.value = window.innerWidth\n  theme.value = localStorage.getItem('theme') ?? 'light'\n})\n\n// ✅ Explicit environment check when you need one outside a hook\nif (typeof window !== 'undefined') { /* client-only */ }\n// In Nuxt:  if (import.meta.client) { … }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;!-- ❌ Different output on server and client → hydration mismatch --&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Rendered at {{ Date.now() }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;div v-if="window.innerWidth &gt; 768"&gt;Desktop&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Starts identical on both sides, updates after mount --&gt;\n  &lt;div v-if="width &gt; 768"&gt;Desktop&lt;/div&gt;\n\n  &lt;!-- ✅ Skip SSR entirely for genuinely browser-only widgets (Nuxt) --&gt;\n  &lt;ClientOnly&gt;\n    &lt;InteractiveMap /&gt;\n    &lt;template #fallback&gt;&lt;MapSkeleton /&gt;&lt;/template&gt;\n  &lt;/ClientOnly&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Nuxt: data fetching that works on both sides automatically --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\n// Runs on the SERVER during SSR, and the result is serialised into the HTML\n// so the client doesn't refetch. This is the piece people hand-roll badly.\nconst { data: product, pending, error, refresh } = await useFetch(\`/api/products/\${id}\`)\n\n// useAsyncData for anything that isn't a plain HTTP GET\nconst { data: stats } = await useAsyncData('stats', () =&gt; computeStats(id))\n\n// SEO — rendered into the served HTML, so crawlers see it\nuseHead({\n  title: () =&gt; product.value?.name,\n  meta: [{ name: 'description', content: () =&gt; product.value?.summary }],\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div v-if="pending"&gt;Loading…&lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;ErrorPanel v-else-if="error" :error="error" /&gt;\n  &lt;ProductDetail v-else :product="product" /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// nuxt.config.ts — rendering mode per route\nexport default defineNuxtConfig({\n  routeRules: {\n    '/': { prerender: true },                        // SSG at build time\n    '/blog/**': { isr: 3600 },                       // static, revalidated hourly\n    '/products/**': { swr: 600 },                    // stale-while-revalidate\n    '/dashboard/**': { ssr: false },                 // pure SPA — behind a login\n    '/api/**': { cors: true },\n  },\n})\n// One app, four rendering strategies — this flexibility is the main\n// practical reason to use Nuxt rather than hand-rolling SSR.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Browser APIs during setup.</strong> <code>window is not defined</code> at render time. Move it into <code>onMounted</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Module-scope state.</strong> Shared across every request in the same process — a genuine cross-user data leak. Create everything per request.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not transferring state.</strong> Server rendered with data, client starts empty, mismatch and re-render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>JSON.stringify</code> for state transfer.</strong> Loses <code>undefined</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>Set</code>. Use <code>devalue</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Non-deterministic render output.</strong> <code>Date.now()</code>, <code>Math.random()</code> and locale-dependent formatting differ between the two renders.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Invalid HTML nesting.</strong> The browser silently corrects a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, so the client tree no longer matches the server's.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>onMounted</code> on the server.</strong> It never runs there, which is both the trap and the tool.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>createApp</code> instead of <code>createSSRApp</code>.</strong> The latter is what enables hydration rather than a fresh render.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Adopting SSR for an app behind a login.</strong> No SEO benefit, and you have taken on a server, per-request rendering cost and a whole class of bugs.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Use Nuxt rather than hand-rolling SSR unless you have a strong reason — the infrastructure is substantial and easy to get subtly wrong.</li>`,
        `<li>Put every browser API in <code>onMounted</code> or behind an explicit environment check.</li>`,
        `<li>Create the app, router and store per request; never at module scope.</li>`,
        `<li>Transfer state with <code>devalue</code> and restore it before mounting.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep the first render deterministic — no timestamps, randomness or viewport branching.</li>`,
        `<li>Wrap genuinely browser-only components in <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code> with a fallback that matches the eventual layout.</li>`,
        `<li>Pick the rendering mode per route: static for marketing, SSR for personalised pages, SPA for dashboards.</li>`,
        `<li>Validate HTML nesting — mismatches from invalid markup are the hardest to spot.</li>`,
        `<li>Choose SSR for a reason: SEO, social previews, or first-paint on slow devices. Otherwise stay a SPA.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is SSR and hydration?"</strong><br>A: SSR means the server runs the Vue app and sends real HTML, so the user sees content before any JavaScript executes — better for SEO, social previews and first paint on slow devices. Hydration is the second half: the same app boots in the browser and, instead of recreating the DOM, walks the existing server-rendered markup and attaches reactivity and event listeners to it. That's why you use <code>createSSRApp</code> — it tells Vue to hydrate rather than render fresh.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What causes a hydration mismatch?"</strong><br>A: The client's first render producing different markup from the server's. Common causes: non-deterministic values like <code>Date.now()</code> or <code>Math.random()</code>; branching on something available on only one side, like <code>window.innerWidth</code> or a client-read cookie; invalid HTML nesting that the browser silently corrects, so the parsed DOM differs from what the server emitted; and most often, state that wasn't transferred — the server rendered with data and the client started empty. Vue warns, throws away the mismatched subtree and re-renders it, so you lose the benefit and may see a flash.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does <code>window is not defined</code> happen, and what's the general rule?"</strong><br>A: Server code runs in Node, which has no DOM globals. The general rule is that browser-only code belongs in <code>onMounted</code>, because that hook never runs on the server — that's not a coincidence, it's the designated client boundary. Where you need a check outside a hook, <code>typeof window !== 'undefined'</code>, or <code>import.meta.client</code> in Nuxt. The same root cause explains why module-scope state is dangerous: module scope is per process, not per request, so one user's data reaches another's render.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does state get from server to client?"</strong><br>A: The server serialises its store state into the HTML — typically <code>window.__INITIAL_STATE__</code> — and the client assigns it to Pinia <em>before</em> mounting, so the first client render matches the server's markup exactly. Serialise with <code>devalue</code> rather than <code>JSON.stringify</code>, which silently loses <code>undefined</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>Set</code>. Nuxt's <code>useFetch</code> and <code>useAsyncData</code> do this automatically, which is a large part of why people use it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When would you NOT use SSR?" (trap)</strong><br>A: When there's no SEO or first-paint case to make. An internal dashboard behind a login is the clearest example — crawlers never see it, users are on good connections and warm caches, and you'd be taking on a Node server, per-request render cost, dual-environment bugs and hydration mismatches for nothing. SSR is a trade, not an upgrade. And where the goal is purely SEO on content that rarely changes, SSG gives you the same benefit with none of the runtime complexity.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>SSR = server renders HTML · <strong>hydration</strong> = client attaches to existing markup (<code>createSSRApp</code>).</li>`,
        `<li>Modes: SSR (per request) · SSG (build time) · ISR (revalidated) · CSR (SPA).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Root cause of every SSR bug: Node has no <code>window</code>/<code>document</code>/<code>localStorage</code>.</strong></li>`,
        `<li><code>onMounted</code>/<code>onUnmounted</code> <strong>never run on the server</strong> → the client boundary.</li>`,
        `<li>Module-scope state = per process, not per request ⇒ <strong>cross-user leak</strong>. Create per request.</li>`,
        `<li>Mismatch causes: non-determinism · one-sided branching · invalid HTML nesting · untransferred state.</li>`,
        `<li>Transfer state with <code>devalue</code>; assign it <strong>before mount</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Nuxt: file routing, <code>useFetch</code>/<code>useAsyncData</code> (auto transfer), <code>&lt;ClientOnly&gt;</code>, per-route <code>routeRules</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>No SEO need ⇒ no SSR. Rarely-changing content ⇒ SSG, not SSR.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Vue 2 to Vue 3: What Changed and How to Migrate',
      slug: 'vue2-to-vue3',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue 2 reached end of life at the end of 2023, so migration is live work at a lot of companies — and interviewers ask about it both to test breadth (you need to know both versions) and to test how you approach large, risky changes. As with the Vuex question, the answer that lands is incremental, not big-bang.</p>`,
        `<p>It is also the cleanest way to demonstrate that you understand <em>why</em> Vue 3 is built the way it is, since almost every breaking change traces back to one of three motivations: the reactivity rewrite, tree-shaking, or removing global state.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>The changes that matter, grouped by what caused them:</p>`,
        `<p><strong>1. Reactivity rewritten (<code>defineProperty</code> → <code>Proxy</code>)</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>Vue.set</code>/<code>Vue.delete</code> are gone — adding and deleting properties just works.</li>`,
        `<li>Array index assignment and <code>length</code> changes are now detected.</li>`,
        `<li>Map and Set are reactive.</li>`,
        `<li>IE11 support dropped, because <code>Proxy</code> cannot be polyfilled.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>2. Global API scoped to an app instance</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>new Vue()</code> → <code>createApp()</code>; <code>Vue.use</code>/<code>Vue.component</code>/<code>Vue.directive</code> → <code>app.*</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>Vue.prototype.$x</code> → <code>app.config.globalProperties.$x</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Multiple independent apps on one page become possible.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>3. Composition API added</strong> — additive, not breaking; the Options API still works.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>4. Template and component changes</strong></p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Multiple root nodes allowed (fragments).</li>`,
        `<li>Filters removed — use computeds or methods.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-model</code> on components: <code>value</code>/<code>input</code> → <code>modelValue</code>/<code>update:modelValue</code>; <code>.sync</code> replaced by named <code>v-model</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-if</code> now has priority over <code>v-for</code> (reversed).</li>`,
        `<li><code>$listeners</code> merged into <code>$attrs</code>; <code>$on</code>/<code>$off</code>/<code>$once</code> removed (no event bus).</li>`,
        `<li><code>&lt;Teleport&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> and multiple <code>v-model</code>s added.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>5. Ecosystem</strong> — Vue Router 4, Vuex 4 (or Pinia), and a whole new build story around Vite.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch10-vue2-to-vue3.svg|Migration map showing the three root causes of Vue 3 breaking changes — Proxy reactivity, per-app global API, and tree-shaking — branching into the specific APIs each one removed or changed|Almost every breaking change traces to one of three causes: the reactivity rewrite, scoping globals to an app, or enabling tree-shaking.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>The migration build</strong> is the key tool, and mentioning it signals real experience. <code>@vue/compat</code> is Vue 3 running in a Vue 2-compatible mode: most Vue 2 code works, and every use of a removed or changed API logs a warning telling you what to fix. You migrate incrementally, turning compatibility flags off feature by feature, then remove the compat layer when the warnings are gone.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>A realistic order:</strong></p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li>Upgrade to the latest Vue 2.7, which backports the Composition API — so you can start writing Vue 3-style code before migrating at all.</li>`,
        `<li>Update dependencies to Vue 3-compatible versions and remove any that are abandoned.</li>`,
        `<li>Switch to <code>@vue/compat</code> and get the app running with warnings.</li>`,
        `<li>Fix warnings by category — global API first, then <code>v-model</code>, then filters, then event bus usage.</li>`,
        `<li>Migrate Vue Router 3 → 4 and Vuex 3 → 4 (or move to Pinia).</li>`,
        `<li>Disable compat flags progressively; remove <code>@vue/compat</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Optionally migrate to Vite, and convert components to <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> opportunistically rather than in one sweep.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p>The last point matters: <strong>rewriting working Options API components to the Composition API is not part of the migration.</strong> Both are supported in Vue 3. Converting them adds risk and buys nothing immediate; do it when you are already changing a file for another reason.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The event bus</strong> is the change with no drop-in replacement — <code>$on</code>/<code>$off</code> are gone deliberately. Each usage needs a real decision: props/emits if the components are adjacent, provide/inject for a subtree, a Pinia store for genuinely global signalling, or a tiny external emitter (<code>mitt</code>) as a stopgap.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── App bootstrap ──\n// Vue 2\nimport Vue from 'vue'\nVue.use(VueRouter)\nVue.component('BaseButton', BaseButton)\nVue.prototype.$http = axios\nnew Vue({ router, store, render: h =&gt; h(App) }).$mount('#app')\n\n// Vue 3\nimport { createApp } from 'vue'\nconst app = createApp(App)\napp.use(router).use(store)\napp.component('BaseButton', BaseButton)\napp.config.globalProperties.$http = axios   // escape hatch; prefer imports\napp.mount('#app')</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Reactivity: the workarounds disappear ──\n// Vue 2\nVue.set(this.obj, 'newKey', value)\nVue.delete(this.obj, 'oldKey')\nthis.items.splice(index, 1, newValue)   // index assignment wasn't detected\nthis.$set(this.arr, 0, 'x')\n\n// Vue 3 — all of this just works\nthis.obj.newKey = value\ndelete this.obj.oldKey\nthis.items[index] = newValue\n// Map and Set are reactive too, which they weren't before</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ── v-model on components ── --&gt;\n&lt;!-- Vue 2 --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nexport default {\n  model: { prop: 'checked', event: 'change' },\n  props: { checked: Boolean },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;!-- Parent: &lt;MyInput v-model=\"x\" :title.sync=\"t\" /&gt; --&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Vue 3 --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nconst model = defineModel()            // 3.4+\nconst title = defineModel('title')     // replaces .sync\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;!-- Parent: &lt;MyInput v-model=\"x\" v-model:title=\"t\" /&gt; --&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ── Filters removed ── --&gt;\n&lt;!-- Vue 2 --&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;{{ price | currency }}&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nexport default { filters: { currency: (v) =&gt; \`₹\${v.toFixed(2)}\` } }\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- Vue 3 — a computed, or a plain imported function --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { formatCurrency } from '@/utils/format'\nconst formattedPrice = computed(() =&gt; formatCurrency(price.value))\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ formattedPrice }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{{ formatCurrency(otherPrice) }}&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;!-- also fine --&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── The event bus has no drop-in replacement ──\n// Vue 2\nexport const bus = new Vue()\nbus.$emit('user-updated', user)\nbus.$on('user-updated', handler)\n\n// Vue 3 — decide per usage:\n// a) Adjacent components → props/emits\n// b) A subtree → provide/inject\n// c) Genuinely app-wide → a Pinia store (preferred)\nconst userStore = useUserStore()\nwatch(() =&gt; userStore.user, handler)\n\n// d) Stopgap during migration → a tiny external emitter\nimport mitt from 'mitt'\nexport const emitter = mitt()\nemitter.emit('user-updated', user)\nemitter.on('user-updated', handler)     // remember to off() on unmount</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── The migration build ──\n// vite.config.js (or webpack alias)\nexport default defineConfig({\n  resolve: {\n    alias: { vue: '@vue/compat' },\n  },\n  plugins: [\n    vue({\n      template: {\n        compilerOptions: {\n          compatConfig: { MODE: 2 },     // start fully Vue 2-compatible\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n})\n\n// Then turn features off one at a time as you fix them:\nimport { configureCompat } from 'vue'\nconfigureCompat({\n  MODE: 3,                     // Vue 3 behaviour by default…\n  COMPONENT_V_MODEL: true,     // …except the ones still to migrate\n  FILTERS: true,\n})\n// When nothing is left, remove the alias and the compat package.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Rewriting to the Composition API as part of the migration.</strong> Two risky changes at once. The Options API works in Vue 3.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Skipping the migration build.</strong> It converts a mass of silent breakage into an actionable warning list.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not auditing dependencies first.</strong> An abandoned Vue 2-only UI library can block the whole migration; find that out before you start.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming the event bus has a replacement.</strong> Each usage needs a design decision.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Missing the <code>v-if</code>/<code>v-for</code> priority reversal.</strong> Code that worked now throws on an undefined loop variable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.sync</code> is gone.</strong> It becomes a named <code>v-model</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Leaving filters in templates.</strong> They fail silently or throw depending on the compat mode.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Router changes.</strong> Vue Router 4 changes route matching (custom regex syntax, the catch-all) and makes navigation return promises; guards use return values.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting IE11 to work.</strong> It cannot — <code>Proxy</code> has no polyfill. If IE support is mandatory, Vue 3 is not an option.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Go to Vue 2.7 first — it backports the Composition API, so new code can already be written in the target style.</li>`,
        `<li>Audit dependencies before anything else; a blocked library changes the plan entirely.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>@vue/compat</code> and work through the warnings by category.</li>`,
        `<li>Migrate infrastructure (router, store, build) before touching components.</li>`,
        `<li>Leave working Options API components alone; convert opportunistically.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle each event-bus usage individually — most become props/emits or a store.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep the test suite green throughout; it is the main safety net for a change of this size.</li>`,
        `<li>Ship in stages behind the compat layer rather than as one enormous release.</li>`,
        `<li>Move to Vite after the migration, not during — one variable at a time.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What are the main differences between Vue 2 and Vue 3?"</strong><br>A: Three root causes. Reactivity moved from <code>Object.defineProperty</code> to <code>Proxy</code>, which removed <code>Vue.set</code>/<code>Vue.delete</code>, made array index assignment and Map/Set reactive, and dropped IE11 since <code>Proxy</code> can't be polyfilled. The global API became per-app — <code>createApp()</code> instead of <code>new Vue()</code>, <code>app.use</code> instead of <code>Vue.use</code> — so multiple isolated apps on a page are possible. And the Composition API was added, which is additive rather than breaking. On top of that: fragments, filters removed, <code>v-model</code> changed to <code>modelValue</code>, <code>v-if</code> now beating <code>v-for</code>, no event bus, and Teleport and Suspense added.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you migrate a large Vue 2 app?"</strong><br>A: Incrementally, with the migration build. First upgrade to 2.7 so the Composition API is available, then audit dependencies — an abandoned Vue 2-only library can block everything and I'd want to know that on day one. Then alias <code>vue</code> to <code>@vue/compat</code>, which runs Vue 3 in Vue 2-compatible mode and warns on every changed API. Work through those warnings by category, migrate Router 3→4 and Vuex 3→4 or Pinia, disable compat flags progressively, and remove the compat layer. Crucially, I would <em>not</em> rewrite Options API components to the Composition API as part of this — that's a separate, optional change.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why was IE11 support dropped?"</strong><br>A: Vue 3's reactivity is built on <code>Proxy</code>, which is not polyfillable — you can't intercept arbitrary property access on an object in ES5. The whole benefit of the rewrite depends on it: detecting property addition and deletion, array index assignment, and Map/Set. Supporting IE would have meant shipping the old <code>defineProperty</code> system alongside it, so it was a deliberate trade for a simpler, more capable core.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What replaces the Vue 2 event bus?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Nothing directly — <code>$on</code>, <code>$off</code> and <code>$once</code> were removed on purpose. A global bus makes data flow untraceable and leaks listeners when components unmount without cleanup. Each usage needs its own decision: props and emits between adjacent components, provide/inject for a subtree, and a Pinia store for anything genuinely app-wide, where a watcher on store state replaces the listener. A small external emitter like <code>mitt</code> works as a migration stopgap, but I'd treat it as debt.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Should you rewrite everything to <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> when migrating?"</strong><br>A: No. The Options API is fully supported in Vue 3 and isn't deprecated, so rewriting working components adds regression risk for no functional gain — and doubles the size of a change that's already risky. I'd migrate the framework, keep the test suite green, and then convert components opportunistically when I'm touching them for another reason. New components get <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> from day one.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Three causes: <strong>Proxy reactivity</strong> · <strong>per-app globals</strong> · <strong>tree-shaking</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Gone: <code>Vue.set</code>/<code>Vue.delete</code> · filters · <code>$on</code>/<code>$off</code> (event bus) · <code>.sync</code> · IE11.</li>`,
        `<li><code>new Vue()</code> → <code>createApp()</code>; <code>Vue.use/component/prototype</code> → <code>app.*</code> / <code>globalProperties</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>v-model</code>: <code>value</code>/<code>input</code> → <code>modelValue</code>/<code>update:modelValue</code>; <code>.sync</code> → <code>v-model:name</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>v-if</code> now beats <code>v-for</code></strong> (reversed) — the loop variable is undefined.</li>`,
        `<li>Added: fragments · Teleport · Suspense · Composition API · multiple <code>v-model</code>s.</li>`,
        `<li><code>@vue/compat</code> = Vue 3 in Vue 2 mode with warnings → migrate feature by feature.</li>`,
        `<li>Order: 2.7 → audit deps → compat → fix by category → router/store → drop compat.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Don't rewrite Options API components</strong> as part of the migration.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Debugging Vue Applications',
      slug: 'debugging',
      order: 4,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Debugging rarely gets a dedicated interview question, but it surfaces inside almost every practical one: "the list isn't updating — what do you check?", "this component renders too often — how do you find out why?", "you get a hydration mismatch warning — where do you start?" A candidate with a systematic answer sounds like someone who has fixed real bugs; a candidate who says "add console.logs" sounds like someone who has not.</p>`,
        `<p>Vue also has a specific advantage worth knowing about: because reactivity is explicit, you can ask the framework directly which dependency triggered a re-render. Very few developers know <code>onRenderTriggered</code> exists.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue bugs cluster into a small number of families, and each has a first thing to check:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Usual cause</th><th>First check</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>UI doesn't update</td><td>Broken reactivity</td><td>Destructuring, reassignment, mutating a raw object</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Wrong row's state after sort</td><td>Missing or index <code>:key</code></td><td>The <code>v-for</code> key</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>DOM value is stale</td><td>Async update queue</td><td>Missing <code>await nextTick()</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Re-renders too often</td><td>Over-broad dependencies</td><td>DevTools timeline, <code>onRenderTriggered</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>null</code> template ref</td><td>Read before mount</td><td>Is it inside <code>onMounted</code>?</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Prop change ignored</td><td>Component reuse or copied prop</td><td>Route param watch, local copy</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Hydration mismatch</td><td>Server/client render differ</td><td>Non-determinism, browser APIs, untransferred state</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>"No active Pinia"</td><td><code>useStore()</code> at module scope</td><td>Where the call sits</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `{{fig:ch10-debugging-map.svg|Decision map from common Vue symptoms to their usual causes and the first thing to check, covering reactivity loss, key bugs, async DOM updates, excess re-renders and hydration mismatches|Most Vue bugs fall into a handful of families — matching the symptom to the family is most of the debugging.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Vue DevTools</strong> is the primary instrument. The Components tab shows the tree with each component's props, state and computeds, and lets you edit values live — the fastest way to test "would this render correctly if the data looked like that?" The Timeline records component renders and events, which answers "what is re-rendering and how often?" And Pinia stores appear with time-travel through state changes.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Reactivity debugging hooks</strong> are the underused feature. <code>onRenderTracked</code> fires when a dependency is <em>collected</em> during render; <code>onRenderTriggered</code> fires when one <em>causes</em> a re-render, and the event object tells you the target object, the key and the operation. That turns "why does this re-render on every keystroke?" from guesswork into a direct answer. <code>watch</code> and <code>computed</code> take <code>onTrack</code>/<code>onTrigger</code> options that do the same for a single watcher.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Error handling.</strong> <code>app.config.errorHandler</code> catches every uncaught component error with an <code>info</code> string naming the hook it came from; <code>onErrorCaptured</code> builds boundaries around risky subtrees; and <code>router.onError</code> catches navigation and dynamic-import failures. Wiring all three means production errors reach your monitoring rather than a user's console.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Source maps</strong> make production stack traces readable. Generating them and uploading to Sentry (rather than serving them publicly) is the usual arrangement.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>The SFC Playground</strong> deserves a mention as a debugging tool: pasting a component in and reading the compiled output resolves a whole class of "why does the template behave like that?" questions.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, computed, watch, onRenderTracked, onRenderTriggered } from 'vue'\n\nconst items = ref([])\nconst query = ref('')\n\n// ── Which dependency caused this re-render? ──\nonRenderTriggered((event) =&gt; {\n  console.log('re-rendered because of:', {\n    key: event.key,            // the property that changed\n    type: event.type,          // 'set' | 'add' | 'delete' | 'clear'\n    target: event.target,      // the reactive object\n    oldValue: event.oldValue,\n    newValue: event.newValue,\n  })\n})\n\n// Which dependencies were collected in the first place?\nonRenderTracked((event) =&gt; console.log('tracked:', event.key))\n\n// ── Same idea, scoped to one watcher or computed ──\nwatch(query, refetch, {\n  onTrack: (e) =&gt; console.log('watcher tracked', e.key),\n  onTrigger: (e) =&gt; console.log('watcher fired because', e.key, e.newValue),\n})\n\nconst filtered = computed(() =&gt; items.value.filter(i =&gt; i.name.includes(query.value)), {\n  onTrigger: (e) =&gt; console.log('computed invalidated by', e.key),\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Global error handling: wire all three ──\n// main.js\napp.config.errorHandler = (err, instance, info) =&gt; {\n  // info names the lifecycle hook or feature the error came from\n  Sentry.captureException(err, {\n    tags: { component: instance?.$options?.name ?? 'unknown', hook: info },\n  })\n  console.error(\`[\${info}]\`, err)\n}\n\n// Warnings (development only) — useful for catching prop-type violations in CI\napp.config.warnHandler = (msg, instance, trace) =&gt; {\n  if (import.meta.env.DEV) console.warn(\`[warn] \${msg}\${trace}\`)\n}\n\n// Navigation and dynamic-import failures\nrouter.onError((error) =&gt; Sentry.captureException(error))\n\n// Unhandled promise rejections escape Vue's handler entirely\nwindow.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (e) =&gt; Sentry.captureException(e.reason))</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- ── An error boundary around a risky subtree ── --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { ref, onErrorCaptured } from 'vue'\n\nconst error = ref(null)\n\nonErrorCaptured((err, instance, info) =&gt; {\n  error.value = { message: err.message, info }\n  report(err, info)\n  return false        // stop it propagating to the global handler\n})\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;div v-if="error" class="error-panel"&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Something went wrong in this section.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;button @click="error = null"&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;slot v-else /&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── The reactivity checklist, in code ──\n\n// ① Destructured a reactive object?\nconst { count } = reactive({ count: 0 })   // ❌ dead\nconst { count } = toRefs(state)            // ✅\n\n// ② Reassigned instead of mutating?\nlet state = reactive({ n: 1 })\nstate = { n: 2 }                            // ❌ effects still watch the old proxy\nObject.assign(state, { n: 2 })              // ✅\n\n// ③ Mutated the raw object?\nconst raw = { n: 1 }; const p = reactive(raw)\nraw.n = 2                                   // ❌ no traps fired\n\n// ④ Watching an object without deep?\nwatch(objRef, cb)                           // ❌ only fires on reassignment\nwatch(() =&gt; objRef.value.field, cb)         // ✅ cheaper and gives a real oldValue\n\n// ⑤ Reading the DOM before it updated?\ncount.value++\nel.textContent                              // ❌ previous render\nawait nextTick(); el.textContent            // ✅\n\n// ⑥ Reading a template ref too early?\nconst el = ref(null)\nel.value.focus()                            // ❌ null during setup\nonMounted(() =&gt; el.value.focus())           // ✅</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── Quick inspection tricks ──\nimport { isRef, isReactive, isProxy, toRaw } from 'vue'\n\nconsole.log(isRef(x), isReactive(x), isProxy(x))\nconsole.log(toRaw(state))        // the plain object, easier to read in the console\n\n// DevTools: select a component in the Components tab, then in the console:\n//   $vm            → the component instance\n//   $vm.someRef    → inspect state directly\n\n// Not sure why a template behaves oddly? Paste it into the SFC Playground\n// (play.vuejs.org) and read the compiled render function.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Debugging by adding logs everywhere.</strong> DevTools and the reactivity hooks answer "what changed" directly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Logging a reactive proxy.</strong> The console shows getters rather than values; log <code>toRaw(state)</code> or a JSON copy.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring development warnings.</strong> Vue's warnings are unusually specific and almost always point at the real problem.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No global error handler.</strong> Production errors reach a user's console and nowhere else.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting unhandled promise rejections.</strong> Vue's error handler does not catch them; add a window listener.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No source maps in production.</strong> Stack traces point at minified code and are useless.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Serving source maps publicly.</strong> Upload them to your monitoring service instead of exposing your source.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming a bug is in Vue.</strong> It is nearly always reactivity scope, keys, or async timing.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Debugging a production build.</strong> Warnings and dev-only checks are stripped; reproduce in development first.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Install Vue DevTools and use the Components and Timeline tabs before reaching for logs.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>onRenderTriggered</code> when a component re-renders more than expected — it names the exact key.</li>`,
        `<li>Wire <code>app.config.errorHandler</code>, <code>router.onError</code> and an <code>unhandledrejection</code> listener on day one.</li>`,
        `<li>Put error boundaries around risky subtrees so one failure does not blank the page.</li>`,
        `<li>Generate source maps and upload them to your monitoring service.</li>`,
        `<li>Work the reactivity checklist in order when the UI does not update: destructuring, reassignment, raw mutation, deep watching, async timing, ref timing.</li>`,
        `<li>Reproduce in the SFC Playground when a template behaves inexplicably — the compiled output usually explains it.</li>`,
        `<li>Fix warnings rather than filtering them out of the console.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A component isn't updating when the data changes. How do you debug it?"</strong><br>A: I'd work a checklist, because it's nearly always one of a handful of causes. Was the reactive object destructured, which copies primitives out of the proxy? Was a <code>reactive</code> variable reassigned rather than mutated, so effects still watch the old proxy? Is something mutating the raw object rather than the proxy? Is it a watcher on an object without <code>deep</code>, so nested changes don't fire? Then I'd check in DevTools whether the state is actually changing — if it is and the DOM isn't, it's a rendering issue; if it isn't, the mutation never reached the reactive object.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A component re-renders far more than expected. How do you find out why?"</strong><br>A: <code>onRenderTriggered</code>. It fires whenever a dependency causes a re-render and hands you the target object, the key, the operation type and the old and new values — so instead of guessing, you get the exact property. The DevTools Timeline gives the same picture at a higher level, showing which components render and how often during an interaction. <code>watch</code> and <code>computed</code> also accept <code>onTrack</code>/<code>onTrigger</code> for the same insight scoped to one of them.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you handle errors in production?"</strong><br>A: Three hooks plus one browser listener. <code>app.config.errorHandler</code> catches every uncaught component error and gives you an <code>info</code> string naming the hook it came from. <code>onErrorCaptured</code> builds boundaries around risky subtrees so one failure shows a fallback rather than blanking the page. <code>router.onError</code> catches navigation and failed dynamic imports — which matters after a deploy, when old chunk URLs 404. And a <code>window.unhandledrejection</code> listener, because Vue's handler doesn't catch those. All of it into Sentry, with source maps uploaded so traces are readable.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You log a reactive object and see a mess of getters. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because you're logging a Proxy, and the console displays the handler rather than the underlying values — and expanding it lazily evaluates getters, which can even be misleading about the values at the time you logged. Log <code>toRaw(state)</code> for the plain object, or a structured clone if you want a snapshot that won't change under you. Vue DevTools shows the resolved values properly, which is usually the better tool anyway.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you debug a hydration mismatch?"</strong><br>A: The warning names the element where the trees diverged, so I start there and ask what could differ between the two renders. Usually one of four things: non-deterministic values like a timestamp or random id; branching on something only one side has, such as viewport width or a client-read cookie; invalid HTML nesting that the browser silently repairs, so the parsed DOM doesn't match what the server emitted; or state that wasn't transferred, so the client rendered with empty data. Comparing the server's raw HTML — via view-source, not the inspector, since that shows the corrected DOM — against the client render usually makes it obvious.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Vue DevTools</strong>: Components (inspect/edit state) · Timeline (renders and events) · Pinia (time-travel).</li>`,
        `<li><code>onRenderTriggered(e)</code> → the exact key that caused a re-render. <code>onRenderTracked</code> → deps collected.</li>`,
        `<li><code>watch</code>/<code>computed</code> accept <code>onTrack</code> / <code>onTrigger</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Errors: <code>app.config.errorHandler</code> · <code>onErrorCaptured</code> · <code>router.onError</code> · <code>window unhandledrejection</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Reactivity checklist: destructured? reassigned? mutated raw? missing <code>deep</code>? missing <code>nextTick</code>? ref before mount?</li>`,
        `<li>Log <code>toRaw(state)</code>, not the proxy.</li>`,
        `<li>Hydration mismatch: non-determinism · one-sided branching · invalid nesting · untransferred state. Compare <strong>view-source</strong>, not the inspector.</li>`,
        `<li>Source maps → upload to Sentry, don't serve publicly.</li>`,
        `<li>Reproduce odd template behaviour in the <strong>SFC Playground</strong>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
